window.LP_BOOK = {"file": "the-little-prince.txt", "text": "The Little Prince\nAntoine de Saint-Exupéry\nTranslated from the French for Book Play (2026)\n\nTo Leon Werth\n\nI ask children to forgive me for dedicating this book to a grown-up. I have a serious excuse: this grown-up is the best friend I have in the world. I have another excuse: this grown-up can understand everything, even books for children. I have a third excuse: this grown-up lives in France, where he is hungry and cold. He badly needs comforting. If all these excuses are not enough, then I am happy to dedicate this book to the child this grown-up once was. All grown-ups were children first. (But few of them remember it.) So I correct my dedication:\n\nTo Leon Werth\nwhen he was a little boy\n\nI\n\nOnce, when I was six years old, I saw a magnificent picture in a book about the untouched forest. The book was called \"Stories from Real Life.\" The picture showed a boa constrictor swallowing a wild animal. Here is a copy of the drawing.\n\n[Picture 01-1: a boa constrictor swallowing a wild animal]\n\nThe book said: \"Boa constrictors swallow their prey whole, without chewing it. After that they can no longer move, and they sleep through the six months it takes them to digest.\"\n\nAfter that I thought hard about adventures in the jungle, and then it was my turn: with a colored pencil, I managed to make my very first drawing. My drawing number 1. It looked like this:\n\n[Picture 01-2: drawing number 1, a boa constrictor digesting an elephant, seen from the outside]\n\nI showed my masterpiece to the grown-ups and asked them if my drawing scared them.\n\nThey answered: \"Why would a hat be scary?\"\n\nMy drawing was not a picture of a hat. It was a picture of a boa constrictor digesting an elephant. So I drew the inside of the boa constrictor, so that the grown-ups could understand. They always need things explained. My drawing number 2 looked like this:\n\n[Picture 01-3: drawing number 2, the inside of a boa constrictor with an elephant in it]\n\nThe grown-ups advised me to put aside my drawings of boa constrictors, from the outside or from the inside, and to take an interest in geography, history, arithmetic and grammar instead. That is how, at the age of six, I gave up a wonderful career as a painter. I had been discouraged by the failure of my drawing number 1 and my drawing number 2. Grown-ups never understand anything by themselves, and it is tiring for children to have to explain things to them again and again.\n\nSo I had to choose another job, and I learned to fly airplanes. I have flown a little bit everywhere in the world. And geography, it's true, has been very useful to me. With one look I could tell China from Arizona. That is very handy if you get lost during the night.\n\nIn this way, over the course of my life, I have had lots of dealings with lots of serious people. I have spent a lot of time among grown-ups. I have seen them up close. It hasn't improved my opinion of them much.\n\nWhenever I met one who seemed to me a little bit clear-headed, I would test that person with my drawing number 1, which I have always kept. I wanted to find out if this was someone who really understood. But the answer was always: \"That's a hat.\" So I would not talk to that person about boa constrictors, or untouched forests, or stars. I would come down to their level. I would talk about bridge, golf, politics and neckties. And the grown-up was very pleased to have met such a sensible man.\n\nII\n\nSo I lived alone, without anyone I could really talk to, until my plane broke down in the Sahara Desert six years ago. Something had broken in my engine. And since I had no mechanic and no passengers with me, I got ready to try to do a difficult repair all by myself. For me it was a matter of life or death. I had barely enough drinking water for eight days.\n\nSo on the first night I fell asleep on the sand, a thousand miles from any place where people live. I was much more alone than a shipwrecked sailor on a raft in the middle of the ocean. So you can imagine my surprise when, at daybreak, a funny little voice woke me up. It said:\n\n\"Please... draw me a sheep!\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Draw me a sheep...\"\n\nI jumped to my feet as if I had been struck by lightning. I rubbed my eyes hard. I looked carefully. And I saw a most extraordinary little fellow, who was studying me gravely. Here is the best portrait of him that I managed to make, later on. But my drawing, of course, is much less charming than the real thing. That is not my fault. The grown-ups had discouraged me in my career as a painter when I was six years old, and I had never learned to draw anything except boa constrictors from the outside and boa constrictors from the inside.\n\n[Picture 02-1: a portrait of the little prince]\n\nSo I stared at this apparition, my eyes round with astonishment. Don't forget that I was a thousand miles from any place where people live. And yet my little fellow did not seem to be lost, or dead tired, or dying of hunger, or dying of thirst, or dying of fear. He looked nothing at all like a child lost in the middle of the desert, a thousand miles from any place where people live. When I finally managed to speak, I said to him:\n\n\"But what are you doing here?\"\n\nAnd then he said it again, very softly, as if it were something very serious:\n\n\"Please... draw me a sheep...\"\n\nWhen a mystery is too overwhelming, you don't dare to disobey. Absurd as it seemed to me, a thousand miles from anywhere people live and in danger of death, I took a sheet of paper and a fountain pen out of my pocket. But then I remembered that I had mostly studied geography, history, arithmetic and grammar, and I told the little fellow (a little grumpily) that I didn't know how to draw. He answered:\n\n\"That doesn't matter. Draw me a sheep.\"\n\nSince I had never drawn a sheep, I drew for him one of the only two drawings I knew how to do. The boa constrictor from the outside. And I was amazed to hear the little fellow answer:\n\n\"No! No! I don't want an elephant inside a boa. A boa is very dangerous, and an elephant takes up far too much room. Where I live, everything is very small. I need a sheep. Draw me a sheep.\"\n\nSo I drew.\n\n[Picture 02-2: a sheep]\n\nHe looked at it carefully, and then:\n\n\"No! That one is already very sick. Make another one.\"\n\nI drew:\n\n[Picture 02-3: a sheep with horns]\n\nMy friend smiled kindly and patiently:\n\n\"You can see for yourself... that's not a sheep, it's a ram. It has horns...\"\n\nSo I did my drawing over again. But it was turned down, just like the ones before:\n\n[Picture 02-4: an old sheep]\n\n\"That one is too old. I want a sheep that will live a long time.\"\n\nSo, running out of patience, because I was in a hurry to start taking my engine apart, I scribbled this drawing.\n\n[Picture 02-5: a box with the sheep inside it]\n\nAnd I tossed out:\n\n\"This is the box. The sheep you want is inside.\"\n\nBut I was very surprised to see my young judge's face light up:\n\n\"That's exactly how I wanted it! Do you think this sheep will need a lot of grass?\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because where I live, everything is very small...\"\n\n\"There will surely be enough. I've given you a very small sheep.\"\n\nHe bent his head over the drawing:\n\n\"Not as small as all that... Look! He's fallen asleep...\"\n\nAnd that is how I came to know the little prince.\n\nIII\n\nIt took me a long time to understand where he came from. The little prince, who asked me lots of questions, never seemed to hear mine. It was words he spoke by chance that, little by little, told me everything. For example, when he first saw my plane (I won't draw my plane; that drawing is much too complicated for me), he asked me:\n\n\"What is that thing over there?\"\n\n\"It's not a thing. It flies. It's an airplane. It's my plane.\"\n\nAnd I was proud to let him know that I could fly. Then he cried out:\n\n\"What! You fell out of the sky!\"\n\n\"Yes,\" I said modestly.\n\n\"Oh! That's funny!...\"\n\nAnd the little prince burst into a very pretty peal of laughter, which annoyed me a lot. I want people to take my troubles seriously. Then he added:\n\n\"So you come from the sky too! Which planet are you from?\"\n\nRight away I caught a glimmer of light in the mystery of his being there, and I asked him sharply:\n\n\"So you come from another planet?\"\n\nBut he did not answer me. He was gently nodding his head as he looked at my plane:\n\n\"It's true that on that thing, you can't have come from very far...\"\n\nAnd he sank into a daydream that lasted a long time. Then, taking my sheep out of his pocket, he became lost in gazing at his treasure.\n\nYou can imagine how curious I was about this half-secret about \"the other planets.\" So I tried hard to find out more:\n\n\"Where do you come from, my little fellow? Where is this 'home' of yours? Where do you want to take my sheep?\"\n\nAfter a thoughtful silence, he answered me:\n\n\"The good thing about the box you gave me is that at night he can use it as a house.\"\n\n\"Of course. And if you're good, I'll also give you a rope to tie him up during the day. And a post.\"\n\nThe offer seemed to shock the little prince:\n\n\"Tie him up? What a strange idea!\"\n\n\"But if you don't tie him up, he'll wander off anywhere, and he'll get lost...\"\n\nAnd my friend burst out laughing again:\n\n\"But where do you think he would go!\"\n\n\"Anywhere. Straight ahead of him...\"\n\nThen the little prince said gravely:\n\n\"That doesn't matter, it's so small where I live!\"\n\nAnd, with a little sadness perhaps, he added:\n\n\"If you just go straight ahead, you can't get very far...\"\n\nIV\n\nSo I had learned a second very important thing: his home planet was hardly bigger than a house!\n\nThat could not surprise me very much. I knew perfectly well that apart from the big planets like Earth, Jupiter, Mars and Venus, which have been given names, there are hundreds of others, and some of them are so small that it is very hard to make them out even through a telescope. When an astronomer discovers one of them, he gives it a number for a name. He calls it, for example, \"Asteroid 3251.\"\n\nI have serious reasons to believe that the planet the little prince came from is Asteroid B-612.\n\n[Picture 04-1: the little prince on Asteroid B-612, a planet hardly bigger than a house]\n\nThis asteroid has been seen through a telescope only once, in 1909, by a Turkish astronomer.\n\nAt the time, he gave a grand presentation of his discovery at an International Astronomy Congress.\n\nBut nobody believed him because of the clothes he wore. Grown-ups are like that.\n\nLuckily for the reputation of Asteroid B-612, a Turkish dictator ordered his people, under pain of death, to dress in the European way. The astronomer gave his presentation again in 1920, wearing a very elegant suit. And this time everybody agreed with him.\n\nIf I have told you these details about Asteroid B-612, and if I have told you its number, it is because of the grown-ups. Grown-ups love numbers. When you tell them about a new friend, they never ask you about the things that really matter. They never say to you: \"What does his voice sound like? What games does he like best? Does he collect butterflies?\" Instead they ask you: \"How old is he? How many brothers does he have? How much does he weigh? How much money does his father make?\" Only then do they think they know him. If you say to grown-ups: \"I saw a beautiful house made of pink bricks, with geraniums in the windows and doves on the roof...\" they just can't picture that house. You have to tell them: \"I saw a house worth a hundred thousand francs.\" Then they exclaim: \"Oh, how pretty!\"\n\nSo if you tell them: \"The proof that the little prince existed is that he was delightful, that he laughed, and that he wanted a sheep. When someone wants a sheep, that proves he exists,\" they will shrug their shoulders and call you a child! But if you tell them: \"The planet he came from is Asteroid B-612,\" then they will be convinced, and they will stop bothering you with their questions. That's how they are. You mustn't hold it against them. Children should be very patient with grown-ups.\n\nBut of course, those of us who understand life don't care a bit about numbers! I would have liked to begin this story the way fairy tales begin. I would have liked to say:\n\n\"Once upon a time there was a little prince who lived on a planet hardly bigger than himself, and who needed a friend...\" For those who understand life, that would have seemed much more true.\n\nBecause I don't like people to read my book carelessly. It makes me so sad to tell these memories. It is already six years since my friend went away with his sheep. If I try to describe him here, it is so that I won't forget him. It is sad to forget a friend. Not everyone has had a friend. And I might become like the grown-ups, who are no longer interested in anything but numbers. So that is one more reason why I bought a box of paints and some pencils. It is hard to start drawing again at my age, when the only things you have ever tried are a boa constrictor from the outside and a boa constrictor from the inside, at the age of six! Of course, I will try to make portraits that look as much like him as possible. But I am not at all sure I will succeed. One drawing works, and the next one looks nothing like him. I also get his size a little wrong. Here the little prince is too big. There he is too small. I am also unsure about the color of his clothes. So I fumble along, trying this way and that, as well as I can. And in the end I will get some more important details wrong. But you will have to forgive me for that. My friend never explained anything. Maybe he thought I was like him. But I, unfortunately, do not know how to see sheep through the sides of boxes. Maybe I am a little like the grown-ups. I must have grown old.\n\nV\n\nEvery day I learned something about the planet, about why he left, about his journey. It came very slowly, as things happened to come up in his thoughts. That is how, on the third day, I learned about the tragedy of the baobabs.\n\nThis time, once again, it was thanks to the sheep, because all of a sudden the little prince asked me, as if he had been seized by a serious doubt:\n\n\"It's true, isn't it, that sheep eat bushes?\"\n\n\"Yes. That's true.\"\n\n\"Oh! I'm glad!\"\n\nI did not understand why it was so important that sheep should eat bushes. But the little prince added:\n\n\"So that means they eat baobabs too?\"\n\nI pointed out to the little prince that baobabs are not bushes but trees as big as churches, and that even if he took a whole herd of elephants home with him, that herd would not be able to finish off a single baobab.\n\nThe idea of the herd of elephants made the little prince laugh:\n\n\"We would have to pile them one on top of the other...\"\n\nBut then he wisely remarked:\n\n\"Before they grow big, baobabs start out small.\"\n\n\"That's right! But why do you want your sheep to eat the little baobabs?\"\n\nHe answered me, \"Well, come on!\" as if it were perfectly obvious. And it took a great deal of hard thinking for me to figure out the problem all by myself.\n\nAnd in fact, on the little prince's planet, as on every planet, there were good plants and bad plants. So there were good seeds from good plants and bad seeds from bad plants. But seeds are invisible. They sleep hidden deep in the ground until one of them suddenly feels like waking up. Then it stretches, and at first it shyly pushes a lovely, harmless little sprout up toward the sun. If it is the sprout of a radish or a rosebush, you can let it grow however it likes. But if it is a bad plant, you must pull it up right away, as soon as you recognize it. Now, there were some terrible seeds on the little prince's planet... they were the seeds of baobabs. The soil of the planet was full of them. And a baobab, if you get to it too late, you can never get rid of it again. It spreads over the whole planet. It drills through it with its roots. And if the planet is too small, and if there are too many baobabs, they make it burst.\n\n\"It's a question of discipline,\" the little prince told me later. \"When you have finished getting yourself washed and dressed in the morning, you must carefully get the planet cleaned up too. You have to make yourself pull up the baobabs regularly, as soon as you can tell them apart from the rosebushes, which they look very much like when they are very young. It is very boring work, but very easy.\"\n\nAnd one day he advised me to put all my effort into a beautiful drawing, so the children where I come from would really get this into their heads. \"If they travel someday,\" he told me, \"it could be useful to them. Sometimes it does no harm to put off your work until later. But when it comes to baobabs, it always ends in disaster. I knew a planet where a lazy man lived. He had ignored three bushes...\"\n\nSo, following the little prince's directions, I drew that planet. I don't much like to sound like a preacher. But the danger of baobabs is so little known, and the risks for anyone who might get lost on an asteroid are so great, that just this once I am setting aside my usual reserve. I say: \"Children! Watch out for baobabs!\" It is to warn my friends about a danger they have been brushing past for a long time without knowing it, as I had myself, that I worked so hard on this drawing. The lesson I was teaching was worth the trouble. Perhaps you will ask: Why are there no other drawings in this book as grand as the drawing of the baobabs? The answer is very simple: I tried, but I could not do it. When I drew the baobabs, I was driven by a feeling that it was urgent.\n\n[Picture 05-1: the planet of a lazy man, taken over by three baobabs]\n\nVI\n\nOh, little prince! Little by little, this is how I came to understand your sad little life. For a long time, your only amusement had been the sweetness of sunsets. I learned this new detail on the morning of the fourth day, when you said to me:\n\n\"I really like sunsets. Let's go and watch a sunset...\"\n\n\"But we have to wait...\"\n\n\"Wait for what?\"\n\n\"Wait for the sun to set.\"\n\nAt first you looked very surprised, and then you laughed at yourself. And you said to me:\n\n\"I keep thinking I'm at home!\"\n\nOf course. When it is noon in the United States, the sun, as everybody knows, is setting over France. If only you could get to France in one minute, you could watch the sun go down. Unfortunately, France is much too far away. But on your tiny planet, all you had to do was move your chair a few steps. And you could watch the evening light fade whenever you wanted...\n\n\"One day, I saw the sun set forty-four times!\"\n\nAnd a little later you added:\n\n\"You know... when you are very sad, you love sunsets...\"\n\n\"So on the day of the forty-three times, you were very sad?\" But the little prince did not answer.\n\nVII\n\nOn the fifth day, once again thanks to the sheep, this secret of the little prince's life was revealed to me. He asked me abruptly, without any warning, as if it were the result of a problem he had been thinking over in silence for a long time:\n\n\"A sheep, if it eats bushes, does it eat flowers too?\"\n\n\"A sheep eats everything it finds.\"\n\n\"Even flowers that have thorns?\"\n\n\"Yes. Even flowers that have thorns.\"\n\n\"Then the thorns, what are they for?\"\n\nI did not know. At that moment I was very busy trying to unscrew a bolt in my engine that was stuck too tight. I was very worried, because my breakdown was starting to look very serious, and my drinking water was running out, which made me fear the worst.\n\n\"The thorns, what are they for?\"\n\nThe little prince never gave up on a question once he had asked it. I was annoyed with my bolt, and I said the first thing that came into my head:\n\n\"Thorns are no use at all. They're just pure meanness on the flowers' part!\"\n\n\"Oh!\"\n\nBut after a silence, he threw back at me, with a sort of bitterness:\n\n\"I don't believe you! Flowers are weak. They are innocent. They comfort themselves however they can. They think their thorns make them frightening...\"\n\nI said nothing. At that moment I was thinking: \"If this bolt still won't give, I'll knock it loose with a hammer.\" The little prince broke into my thoughts again:\n\n\"And you really believe that flowers...\"\n\n\"No! No! I don't believe anything! I just said the first thing that came into my head. I am busy with serious things!\"\n\nHe stared at me in amazement.\n\n\"Serious things!\"\n\nHe saw me there, hammer in hand, my fingers black with engine grease, bending over something that looked very ugly to him.\n\n\"You talk like the grown-ups!\"\n\nThat made me feel a little ashamed. But, without mercy, he went on:\n\n\"You mix everything up... you get it all confused!\"\n\nHe was really very angry. He tossed his golden hair in the wind:\n\n\"I know a planet where there is a gentleman with a bright red face. He has never smelled a flower. He has never looked at a star. He has never loved anyone. He has never done anything but add up numbers. And all day long he keeps saying, just like you: 'I am a serious man! I am a serious man!' and it makes him puff up with pride. But he isn't a man, he's a mushroom!\"\n\n\"A what?\"\n\n\"A mushroom!\"\n\nThe little prince was now quite pale with anger.\n\n\"For millions of years, flowers have been making thorns. For millions of years, sheep have been eating flowers all the same. And it isn't serious to try to understand why flowers go to so much trouble to make thorns that are never any use at all? The war between the sheep and the flowers isn't important? It isn't more serious and more important than a fat red gentleman's sums? And if I know a flower that is the only one of its kind in the world, that grows nowhere except on my planet, and that a little sheep can destroy in one bite, just like that, one morning, without even realizing what he is doing, that isn't important?\"\n\nHe turned red, then went on:\n\n\"If someone loves a flower, and there is only one like it in all the millions and millions of stars, that is enough to make him happy when he looks at them. He says to himself: 'My flower is up there somewhere...' But if the sheep eats the flower, for him it is as if, all of a sudden, every star went dark! And that isn't important!\"\n\nHe could not say anything more. All at once he burst into sobs. Night had fallen. I had dropped my tools. I did not care at all anymore about my hammer, my bolt, thirst or death. On a star, on a planet, on mine, the Earth, there was a little prince who needed comforting! I took him in my arms. I rocked him. I said to him: \"The flower you love is not in danger... I'll draw a muzzle for your sheep... I'll draw a suit of armor for your flower... I...\" I didn't really know what to say. I felt very clumsy. I didn't know how to reach him, where to find him again... The land of tears is such a mysterious place!\n\nVIII\n\nVery soon I came to know this flower better. On the little prince's planet there had always been very simple flowers, decorated with a single ring of petals, that took up hardly any room and bothered nobody. They would appear one morning in the grass, and then in the evening they would fade out. But this one had sprouted one day from a seed that had blown in from who knows where, and the little prince had kept a very close eye on this little shoot, which did not look like the other shoots. It might have been a new kind of baobab. But the little bush soon stopped growing and began to get a flower ready. The little prince, who watched an enormous bud take shape, felt sure that something wonderful would come out of it. But the flower went on and on getting ready to be beautiful, safe inside her green room. She chose her colors with care. She dressed slowly, fitting her petals on one by one. She did not want to come out all crumpled like the poppies. She wanted to appear only in the full glow of her beauty. Oh, yes! She was very vain about her looks! So her mysterious dressing had lasted for days and days. And then one morning, exactly at sunrise, she had shown herself.\n\nAnd she, who had worked so carefully, said with a yawn:\n\n\"Oh! I've only just woken up... I'm so sorry... My hair is still all messy...\"\n\nThen the little prince could not hold back his admiration:\n\n\"How beautiful you are!\"\n\n\"Aren't I?\" the flower answered softly. \"And I was born at the same moment as the sun...\"\n\nThe little prince could tell that she was not very modest, but she was so touching!\n\n\"I believe it is time for breakfast,\" she soon added. \"Would you be so kind as to think of me...\"\n\nAnd the little prince, very embarrassed, went and fetched a watering can full of cool water and served the flower.\n\n[Picture 08-1: the little prince watering a proud red rose with four small thorns on his tiny planet]\n\nSo, with her rather touchy pride, she had soon begun to torment him. One day, for example, talking about her four thorns, she had said to the little prince:\n\n\"Let them come, the tigers, with their claws!\"\n\n\"There are no tigers on my planet,\" the little prince had objected, \"and anyway, tigers don't eat grass.\"\n\n\"I am not grass,\" the flower had answered softly.\n\n\"I'm sorry...\"\n\n\"I'm not a bit afraid of tigers, but I can't stand drafts. You wouldn't have a screen, would you?\"\n\n\"Can't stand drafts... that's bad luck, for a plant,\" the little prince had remarked. \"This flower is very complicated...\"\n\n\"In the evening you will put me under a glass globe. It is very cold where you live. Things are badly arranged here. Where I come from...\"\n\nBut she had stopped herself. She had come as a seed. She could not have known anything about other worlds. Ashamed at being caught getting ready to tell such a silly lie, she had coughed two or three times, to put the little prince in the wrong:\n\n\"That screen?...\"\n\n\"I was going to get it, but you were talking to me!\"\n\nThen she had made herself cough harder, so that he would feel sorry anyway.\n\nAnd so the little prince, even though he loved her and meant well, had soon come to doubt her. He had taken unimportant words seriously, and he had become very unhappy.\n\n\"I shouldn't have listened to her,\" he told me one day. \"You must never listen to flowers. You should look at them and smell them. Mine filled my planet with her perfume, but I didn't know how to enjoy it. That business about the claws, which annoyed me so much, should have made me feel tender toward her...\"\n\nHe also told me:\n\n\"Back then I didn't understand anything! I should have judged her by what she did, not by what she said. She gave me her perfume and her light. I never should have run away! I should have guessed the tenderness behind her poor little tricks. Flowers are so full of contradictions! But I was too young to know how to love her.\"\n\nIX\n\nI believe that for his escape he took advantage of a flock of wild birds that were migrating. On the morning he left, he put his planet in good order. He carefully swept out his active volcanoes. He owned two active volcanoes, and they were very handy for heating up his breakfast in the morning. He also owned one extinct volcano. But, as he said, \"You never know!\" So he swept out the extinct volcano too. When they are well swept, volcanoes burn gently and steadily, without erupting. Volcanic eruptions are like chimney fires. Of course, here on our Earth we are much too small to sweep out our volcanoes. That is why they cause us so much trouble.\n\n[Picture 09-1: the little prince carefully sweeping out one of his volcanoes]\n\nWith a little sadness, the little prince also pulled up the last baobab shoots. He believed he would never come back. But on that morning all these familiar chores seemed extremely sweet to him. And when he watered the flower for the last time and got ready to put her safely under her glass globe, he found that he wanted to cry.\n\n\"Goodbye,\" he said to the flower.\n\nBut she did not answer him.\n\n\"Goodbye,\" he said again.\n\nThe flower coughed. But it was not because of her cold.\n\n\"I have been foolish,\" she said to him at last. \"I'm sorry. Try to be happy.\"\n\nHe was surprised that she did not scold him. He stood there, completely thrown, holding the glass globe in the air. He did not understand this calm gentleness.\n\n\"Of course I love you,\" the flower told him. \"You never knew it, and that was my fault. It doesn't matter at all. But you were just as foolish as I was. Try to be happy... Leave that globe alone. I don't want it anymore.\"\n\n\"But the wind...\"\n\n\"My cold isn't that bad... The cool night air will do me good. I am a flower.\"\n\n\"But the animals...\"\n\n\"I have to put up with two or three caterpillars if I want to get to know the butterflies. They say butterflies are so beautiful. Otherwise, who will come to visit me? You will be far away. As for the big animals, I'm not afraid of them. I have my claws.\"\n\nAnd she innocently showed him her four thorns. Then she added:\n\n\"Don't hang around like this. It's annoying. You have decided to leave. Go away.\"\n\nFor she did not want him to see her cry. She was such a proud flower...\n\nX\n\nHe found himself in the neighborhood of asteroids 325, 326, 327, 328, 329 and 330. So he started by visiting them, to look for something to do and to learn things.\n\nThe first one was the home of a king. Dressed in purple and ermine, the king sat on a throne that was very simple and yet very grand.\n\n[Picture 10-1: a king in a purple and ermine robe, sitting on a simple throne on a tiny planet]\n\n\"Ah! Here is a subject!\" cried the king when he caught sight of the little prince.\n\nAnd the little prince wondered:\n\n\"How can he recognize me when he has never seen me before?\"\n\nHe did not know that for kings the world is very simple. All men are subjects.\n\n\"Come closer, so that I can see you better,\" said the king, who was very proud to be a king for somebody at last.\n\nThe little prince looked around for somewhere to sit, but the whole planet was taken up by the magnificent ermine robe. So he stayed standing, and since he was tired, he yawned.\n\n\"It is against the rules of the court to yawn in front of a king,\" the monarch told him. \"I forbid you to do it.\"\n\n\"I can't help it,\" answered the little prince, very embarrassed. \"I've made a long journey and I haven't slept...\"\n\n\"Then,\" said the king, \"I order you to yawn. I haven't seen anyone yawn for years. To me, yawns are a curious thing. Come on! Yawn again. That is an order.\"\n\n\"That makes me shy... I can't anymore...\" said the little prince, turning all red.\n\n\"Hm! Hm!\" answered the king. \"Then I... I order you sometimes to yawn and sometimes to...\"\n\nHe was stammering a little and seemed annoyed.\n\nFor what mattered most to the king was that his authority be respected. He would not put up with disobedience. He was an absolute monarch. But since he was very kind, the orders he gave were reasonable.\n\n\"If I ordered,\" he would often say, \"if I ordered a general to turn himself into a seabird, and if the general did not obey, it would not be the general's fault. It would be my fault.\"\n\n\"May I sit down?\" the little prince asked shyly.\n\n\"I order you to sit down,\" answered the king, and he grandly pulled back a fold of his ermine robe.\n\nBut the little prince was puzzled. The planet was tiny. What could the king possibly rule over?\n\n\"Sire,\" he said to him, \"... please forgive me for asking you a question...\"\n\n\"I order you to ask me a question,\" the king said quickly.\n\n\"Sire... what do you rule over?\"\n\n\"Over everything,\" answered the king, very simply.\n\n\"Over everything?\"\n\nWith a small, modest gesture the king pointed to his planet, the other planets and the stars.\n\n\"Over all that?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Over all that...\" answered the king.\n\nFor he was not only an absolute monarch, he was a monarch of the whole universe.\n\n\"And the stars obey you?\"\n\n\"Of course,\" the king told him. \"They obey at once. I do not put up with lack of discipline.\"\n\nSuch power amazed the little prince. If he had had it himself, he could have watched not forty-four but seventy-two, or even a hundred, or even two hundred sunsets in the same day, without ever having to move his chair! And because he felt a little sad as he remembered his small planet that he had left behind, he gathered his courage and asked the king a favor:\n\n\"I would like to see a sunset... Please do this for me... Order the sun to set...\"\n\n\"If I ordered a general to fly from one flower to another like a butterfly, or to write a tragic play, or to turn himself into a seabird, and if the general did not carry out the order he was given, which of us would be in the wrong, he or I?\"\n\n\"You would,\" the little prince said firmly.\n\n\"Exactly. You must ask of each person what each person is able to give,\" the king went on. \"Authority rests first of all on reason. If you order your people to go and throw themselves into the sea, they will start a revolution. I have the right to demand obedience because my orders are reasonable.\"\n\n\"Then what about my sunset?\" the little prince reminded him, for he never forgot a question once he had asked it.\n\n\"You shall have your sunset. I will demand it. But, in my wisdom as a ruler, I will wait until the conditions are right.\"\n\n\"When will that be?\" asked the little prince.\n\n\"Hem! Hem!\" answered the king, who first looked in a big calendar. \"Hem! Hem! That will be around... around... that will be this evening at about seven-forty! And you will see how well I am obeyed.\"\n\nThe little prince yawned. He was sorry to have missed his sunset. And besides, he was already getting a little bored:\n\n\"There's nothing more for me to do here,\" he said to the king. \"I'm going to set off again!\"\n\n\"Don't go,\" answered the king, who was so proud to have a subject. \"Don't go. I will make you a minister!\"\n\n\"A minister of what?\"\n\n\"Of... of justice!\"\n\n\"But there is nobody to judge!\"\n\n\"You never know,\" the king told him. \"I haven't yet been all the way around my kingdom. I am very old, there is no room for a carriage, and walking tires me.\"\n\n\"Oh! But I've already looked,\" said the little prince, leaning over to take one more glance at the other side of the planet. \"There is nobody over there either...\"\n\n\"Then you shall judge yourself,\" the king answered him. \"That is the hardest thing of all. It is much harder to judge yourself than to judge others. If you manage to judge yourself well, then you are truly wise.\"\n\n\"I can judge myself anywhere,\" said the little prince. \"I don't need to live here to do that.\"\n\n\"Hem! Hem!\" said the king. \"I rather think there is an old rat somewhere on my planet. I hear him at night. You can judge this old rat. From time to time you will sentence him to death. That way his life will depend on your justice. But you will pardon him every time, so as not to waste him. He is the only one we have.\"\n\n\"I don't like sentencing anyone to death,\" answered the little prince, \"and I really think I'll be going now.\"\n\n\"No,\" said the king.\n\nBut the little prince, who had finished getting ready, did not want to hurt the old monarch's feelings:\n\n\"If Your Majesty wishes to be obeyed promptly, you could give me a reasonable order. You could order me, for example, to leave within one minute. It seems to me that the conditions are right...\"\n\nSince the king said nothing, the little prince hesitated at first, and then, with a sigh, he set off.\n\n\"I make you my ambassador!\" the king hurried to call out.\n\nHe had a great air of authority.\n\nGrown-ups are very strange, the little prince said to himself as he traveled on.\n\nXI\n\nThe second planet was the home of a vain man:\n\n\"Ah! Ah! Here comes a visit from an admirer!\" the vain man cried out from far away as soon as he caught sight of the little prince.\n\nFor to vain men, all other people are admirers.\n\n[Picture 11-1: a vain man wearing a strange hat]\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the little prince. \"That's a funny hat you have.\"\n\n\"It's for bowing,\" the vain man answered him. \"It's for bowing when people cheer for me. Unfortunately, nobody ever comes this way.\"\n\n\"Oh, really?\" said the little prince, who did not understand.\n\n\"Clap your hands together,\" the vain man advised him.\n\nThe little prince clapped his hands together. The vain man bowed modestly, lifting his hat.\n\n\"This is more fun than the visit to the king,\" the little prince said to himself. And he started clapping his hands together again. The vain man started bowing again, lifting his hat.\n\nAfter five minutes of this, the little prince grew tired of how boring the game was:\n\n\"And what do I have to do,\" he asked, \"to make the hat fall off?\"\n\nBut the vain man did not hear him. Vain men never hear anything but praise.\n\n\"Do you really admire me very much?\" he asked the little prince.\n\n\"What does 'admire' mean?\"\n\n\"To admire means to agree that I am the handsomest, the best-dressed, the richest and the cleverest man on the planet.\"\n\n\"But you're all alone on your planet!\"\n\n\"Do me this favor. Admire me anyway!\"\n\n\"I admire you,\" said the little prince, with a little shrug of his shoulders, \"but why should that matter to you at all?\"\n\nAnd the little prince went away.\n\nGrown-ups really are very odd, he simply said to himself as he traveled on.\n\nXII\n\nThe next planet was the home of a drinker. This visit was very short, but it filled the little prince with deep sadness:\n\n\"What are you doing there?\" he said to the drinker, whom he found sitting in silence in front of a collection of empty bottles and a collection of full bottles.\n\n[Picture 12-1: a drinker sitting in silence in front of empty bottles and full bottles]\n\n\"I'm drinking,\" answered the drinker, with a gloomy look.\n\n\"Why are you drinking?\" the little prince asked him.\n\n\"To forget,\" answered the drinker.\n\n\"To forget what?\" asked the little prince, who already felt sorry for him.\n\n\"To forget that I'm ashamed,\" the drinker admitted, hanging his head.\n\n\"Ashamed of what?\" asked the little prince, who wanted to help him.\n\n\"Ashamed of drinking!\" the drinker finished, and he shut himself up in silence for good.\n\nAnd the little prince went away, puzzled.\n\nGrown-ups really are very, very odd, he said to himself as he traveled on.\n\nXIII\n\nThe fourth planet belonged to a businessman. This man was so busy that he did not even raise his head when the little prince arrived.\n\n[Picture 13-1: a businessman busy adding up numbers, with an unlit cigarette]\n\n\"Good morning,\" the little prince said to him. \"Your cigarette has gone out.\"\n\n\"Three and two make five. Five and seven, twelve. Twelve and three, fifteen. Good morning. Fifteen and seven, twenty-two. Twenty-two and six, twenty-eight. No time to light it again. Twenty-six and five, thirty-one. Phew! So that makes five hundred one million, six hundred twenty-two thousand, seven hundred thirty-one.\"\n\n\"Five hundred million what?\"\n\n\"Huh? Are you still there? Five hundred one million... I can't remember... I have so much work to do! I am a serious man. I don't waste my time on nonsense! Two and five, seven...\"\n\n\"Five hundred million what?\" the little prince said again. Never in his life had he given up on a question once he had asked it.\n\nThe businessman raised his head:\n\n\"In the fifty-four years that I have lived on this planet, I have been disturbed only three times. The first time was twenty-two years ago, by a beetle that had dropped in from heaven knows where. It made a terrible noise, and I made four mistakes in one sum. The second time was eleven years ago, by an attack of rheumatism. I don't get enough exercise. I have no time to stroll around. I am a serious man. The third time... is right now! As I was saying, five hundred one million...\"\n\n\"Million what?\"\n\nThe businessman understood that there was no hope of being left in peace:\n\n\"Millions of those little things you sometimes see in the sky.\"\n\n\"Flies?\"\n\n\"No, no. Little things that shine.\"\n\n\"Bees?\"\n\n\"No, no. Little golden things that make lazy people daydream. But I am a serious man! I have no time for daydreaming.\"\n\n\"Oh! Stars?\"\n\n\"That's it. Stars.\"\n\n\"And what do you do with five hundred million stars?\"\n\n\"Five hundred one million, six hundred twenty-two thousand, seven hundred thirty-one. I am a serious man. I am exact.\"\n\n\"And what do you do with these stars?\"\n\n\"What do I do with them?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Nothing. I own them.\"\n\n\"You own the stars?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"But I have already met a king who...\"\n\n\"Kings don't own things. They 'rule over' them. That is very different.\"\n\n\"And what good does it do you to own the stars?\"\n\n\"It makes me rich.\"\n\n\"And what good does it do you to be rich?\"\n\n\"It lets me buy more stars, if anyone finds some.\"\n\nThat man, the little prince said to himself, reasons a bit like my drunkard.\n\nStill, he asked more questions:\n\n\"How can anyone own the stars?\"\n\n\"Who do they belong to?\" the businessman snapped back grumpily.\n\n\"I don't know. To nobody.\"\n\n\"Then they belong to me, because I was the first to think of it.\"\n\n\"Is that enough?\"\n\n\"Of course. When you find a diamond that belongs to nobody, it is yours. When you find an island that belongs to nobody, it is yours. When you are the first to have an idea, you get a patent for it: it is yours. And I own the stars, because nobody before me ever thought of owning them.\"\n\n\"That's true,\" said the little prince. \"And what do you do with them?\"\n\n\"I manage them. I count them and count them again,\" said the businessman. \"It is hard work. But I am a serious man!\"\n\nThe little prince was still not satisfied.\n\n\"If I own a scarf, I can put it around my neck and take it with me. If I own a flower, I can pick my flower and take it with me. But you can't pick the stars!\"\n\n\"No, but I can put them in the bank.\"\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"It means that I write the number of my stars on a little piece of paper. And then I lock that paper in a drawer.\"\n\n\"And that's all?\"\n\n\"That's enough!\"\n\nThat's funny, thought the little prince. It's rather poetic. But it isn't very serious.\n\nThe little prince had ideas about serious things that were very different from the ideas of grown-ups.\n\n\"I own a flower,\" he went on, \"and I water her every day. I own three volcanoes, and I sweep them out every week. I sweep out the extinct one too. You never know. It is useful to my volcanoes, and useful to my flower, that I own them. But you are not useful to the stars...\"\n\nThe businessman opened his mouth, but he could not find anything to say, and the little prince went away.\n\nGrown-ups really are quite extraordinary, he simply said to himself as he traveled on.\n\nXIV\n\nThe fifth planet was very strange. It was the smallest of all. There was just enough room on it for a street lamp and a lamplighter. The little prince could not work out what use a street lamp and a lamplighter could be, somewhere up in the sky, on a planet with no house and no people. Still, he said to himself:\n\n\"Maybe this man is absurd. But he is less absurd than the king, the vain man, the businessman and the drinker. At least his work has some meaning. When he lights his lamp, it is as if he were bringing one more star to life, or a flower. When he puts out his lamp, that sends the flower or the star to sleep. It is a very pretty job. It is truly useful, since it is pretty.\"\n\nWhen he reached the planet, he greeted the lamplighter respectfully:\n\n\"Good morning. Why did you just put out your lamp?\"\n\n\"Those are the orders,\" answered the lamplighter. \"Good morning.\"\n\n\"What are the orders?\"\n\n\"To put out my lamp. Good evening.\"\n\nAnd he lit it again.\n\n\"But why did you just light it again?\"\n\n\"Those are the orders,\" answered the lamplighter.\n\n\"I don't understand,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"There is nothing to understand,\" said the lamplighter. \"Orders are orders. Good morning.\"\n\nAnd he put out his lamp.\n\nThen he wiped his forehead with a red-checked handkerchief.\n\n[Picture 14-1: the lamplighter wiping his forehead beside his street lamp]\n\n\"I have a terrible job here. It used to make sense. I put the lamp out in the morning and lit it in the evening. I had the rest of the day to rest, and the rest of the night to sleep...\"\n\n\"And since then, the orders have changed?\"\n\n\"The orders have not changed,\" said the lamplighter. \"That's just the trouble! Year after year the planet has turned faster and faster, and the orders have not changed!\"\n\n\"So?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"So now that it turns all the way around once a minute, I don't have a single second of rest. I light it and put it out once every minute!\"\n\n\"That's funny! A day on your planet lasts one minute!\"\n\n\"It isn't funny at all,\" said the lamplighter. \"We have already been talking together for a month.\"\n\n\"A month?\"\n\n\"Yes. Thirty minutes. Thirty days! Good evening.\"\n\nAnd he lit his lamp again.\n\nThe little prince watched him, and he grew fond of this lamplighter who was so faithful to his orders. He remembered the sunsets that he himself used to go looking for, back home, by moving his chair. He wanted to help his friend:\n\n\"You know... I know a way you could rest whenever you want...\"\n\n\"I always want to,\" said the lamplighter.\n\nFor a person can be faithful and lazy at the same time.\n\nThe little prince went on:\n\n\"Your planet is so small that you can walk around it in three strides. You only have to walk slowly enough to stay in the sun all the time. When you want to rest, you will walk... and the day will last as long as you like.\"\n\n\"That doesn't do me much good,\" said the lamplighter. \"What I love most in life is sleeping.\"\n\n\"That's bad luck,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"That's bad luck,\" said the lamplighter. \"Good morning.\"\n\nAnd he put out his lamp.\n\n\"That man,\" the little prince said to himself as he traveled on, \"that man would be looked down on by all the others, by the king, by the vain man, by the drinker, by the businessman. And yet he is the only one who doesn't seem ridiculous to me. Maybe it is because he takes care of something other than himself.\"\n\nHe gave a sigh of regret and said to himself again:\n\n\"That man is the only one I could have made my friend. But his planet is really too small. There isn't room for two...\"\n\nWhat the little prince did not dare admit to himself was that he was sorry to leave this blessed planet above all because of its one thousand four hundred and forty sunsets every twenty-four hours!\n\nXV\n\nThe sixth planet was ten times bigger. An old gentleman lived there who wrote enormous books.\n\n\"Well, look at that! Here is an explorer!\" he cried when he caught sight of the little prince.\n\nThe little prince sat down on the table and caught his breath a little. He had already traveled so much!\n\n\"Where do you come from?\" the old gentleman asked him.\n\n\"What is this big book?\" said the little prince. \"What do you do here?\"\n\n\"I am a geographer,\" said the old gentleman.\n\n[Picture 15-1: an old geographer at a desk with an enormous open book, and the little prince sitting on the table]\n\n\"What is a geographer?\"\n\n\"He is a scholar who knows where the seas, the rivers, the towns, the mountains and the deserts are.\"\n\n\"Now that is very interesting,\" said the little prince. \"At last, a real job!\" And he glanced around him at the geographer's planet. He had never before seen a planet so grand.\n\n\"Your planet is very beautiful. Does it have any oceans?\"\n\n\"I have no way of knowing,\" said the geographer.\n\n\"Oh!\" (The little prince was disappointed.) \"And mountains?\"\n\n\"I have no way of knowing,\" said the geographer.\n\n\"And towns and rivers and deserts?\"\n\n\"I have no way of knowing that either,\" said the geographer.\n\n\"But you are a geographer!\"\n\n\"That's right,\" said the geographer, \"but I am not an explorer. I have no explorers at all. It is not the geographer who goes out to count the towns, the rivers, the mountains, the seas, the oceans and the deserts. The geographer is too important to go wandering about. He does not leave his desk. But he receives the explorers there. He asks them questions, and he writes down what they remember. And if what one of them remembers seems interesting to him, the geographer looks into that explorer's character.\"\n\n\"Why is that?\"\n\n\"Because an explorer who told lies would cause disasters in the geography books. And so would an explorer who drank too much.\"\n\n\"Why is that?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Because drunkards see double. Then the geographer would write down two mountains where there is only one.\"\n\n\"I know someone,\" said the little prince, \"who would make a bad explorer.\"\n\n\"That's possible. So, when the explorer's character seems good, someone looks into his discovery.\"\n\n\"Someone goes to see it?\"\n\n\"No. That is too complicated. But the explorer has to supply proof. If it is the discovery of a big mountain, for example, he has to bring back big stones from it.\"\n\nSuddenly the geographer grew excited.\n\n\"But you—you come from far away! You are an explorer! You shall describe your planet to me!\"\n\nAnd the geographer opened his big register and sharpened his pencil. Explorers' reports are written down in pencil first. Only when the explorer has supplied proof are they written in ink.\n\n\"Well?\" asked the geographer.\n\n\"Oh, where I live,\" said the little prince, \"it isn't very interesting, it's very small. I have three volcanoes. Two active volcanoes and one extinct volcano. But you never know.\"\n\n\"You never know,\" said the geographer.\n\n\"I also have a flower.\"\n\n\"We do not write down flowers,\" said the geographer.\n\n\"Why not? It's the prettiest thing!\"\n\n\"Because flowers are ephemeral.\"\n\n\"What does 'ephemeral' mean?\"\n\n\"Geography books,\" said the geographer, \"are the most serious of all books. They never go out of date. It is very rare for a mountain to move. It is very rare for an ocean to run dry. We write about things that last forever.\"\n\n\"But extinct volcanoes can wake up again,\" the little prince broke in. \"What does 'ephemeral' mean?\"\n\n\"Whether volcanoes are extinct or awake, it is all the same to us,\" said the geographer. \"What counts for us is the mountain. It does not change.\"\n\n\"But what does 'ephemeral' mean?\" repeated the little prince, who had never once in his life given up on a question after he had asked it.\n\n\"It means 'in danger of soon disappearing.'\"\n\n\"My flower is in danger of soon disappearing?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\n\"My flower is ephemeral,\" the little prince said to himself, \"and she has only four thorns to defend herself against the world! And I left her all alone at home!\"\n\nThat was the first time he felt regret. But he took heart again:\n\n\"Where would you advise me to go and visit?\" he asked.\n\n\"The planet Earth,\" the geographer answered. \"It has a good reputation...\"\n\nAnd the little prince went away, thinking about his flower.\n\nXVI\n\nSo the seventh planet was the Earth.\n\nThe Earth is not just any planet! It has one hundred and eleven kings (not forgetting, of course, the African kings), seven thousand geographers, nine hundred thousand businessmen, seven and a half million drunkards, three hundred and eleven million vain men—that is, about two billion grown-ups.\n\nTo give you an idea of how big the Earth is, I will tell you that before electricity was invented, a real army of four hundred and sixty-two thousand five hundred and eleven lamplighters had to be kept at work across all six continents.\n\nSeen from a little way off, it made a splendid sight. The movements of this army were arranged like those of a ballet at the opera. First came the turn of the lamplighters of New Zealand and Australia. Then these, once they had lit their lamps, went off to sleep. Next the lamplighters of China and Siberia stepped into the dance. Then they, too, slipped away into the wings. Then came the turn of the lamplighters of Russia and India. Then those of Africa and Europe. Then those of South America. Then those of North America. And they never came on stage in the wrong order. It was magnificent.\n\nOnly the lamplighter of the single street lamp at the North Pole, and his fellow lamplighter of the single street lamp at the South Pole, led lives of idleness and ease: they worked twice a year.\n\nXVII\n\nWhen you try to be witty, you sometimes end up telling a small lie. I have not been very honest with you about the lamplighters. I may be giving a false idea of our planet to people who do not know it. Human beings take up very little room on the Earth. If the two billion people who live on the Earth stood up, packed a little close together, as for a public meeting, they could easily fit into a town square twenty miles long and twenty miles wide. You could pile all of humanity onto the smallest little island in the Pacific.\n\nGrown-ups, of course, will not believe you. They imagine that they take up a great deal of room. They think they are as important as baobabs. So you should advise them to do the arithmetic. They love numbers: they will enjoy it. But don't waste your time on that dull task. There is no need. You trust me.\n\nSo the little prince, once he was on Earth, was very surprised not to see anyone. He was already afraid he had come to the wrong planet, when a ring the color of the moon stirred in the sand.\n\n[Picture 17-1: the little prince on a desert stone at night, talking to a thin yellow snake coiled in the sand]\n\n\"Good night,\" said the little prince, just in case.\n\n\"Good night,\" said the snake.\n\n\"What planet have I landed on?\" asked the little prince.\n\n\"On the Earth, in Africa,\" answered the snake.\n\n\"Oh!... So there is nobody on the Earth?\"\n\n\"This is the desert. There is nobody in deserts. The Earth is big,\" said the snake.\n\nThe little prince sat down on a stone and raised his eyes to the sky:\n\n\"I wonder,\" he said, \"whether the stars are lit up so that each of us can find his own again someday. Look at my planet. It is right above us... But how far away it is!\"\n\n\"It is beautiful,\" said the snake. \"What have you come here for?\"\n\n\"I am having trouble with a flower,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Ah!\" said the snake.\n\nAnd they were both silent.\n\n\"Where are the people?\" the little prince asked at last. \"It's a little lonely in the desert...\"\n\n\"It is lonely among people, too,\" said the snake.\n\nThe little prince looked at him for a long time:\n\n\"You are a funny animal,\" he said at last, \"as thin as a finger...\"\n\n\"But I am more powerful than the finger of a king,\" said the snake.\n\nThe little prince smiled:\n\n\"You aren't very powerful... you don't even have feet... you can't even travel...\"\n\n\"I can carry you farther away than a ship could,\" said the snake.\n\nHe wound himself around the little prince's ankle, like a gold bracelet:\n\n\"Whoever I touch, I send back to the earth he came from,\" he went on. \"But you are pure, and you come from a star...\"\n\nThe little prince did not answer.\n\n\"I feel sorry for you, so weak, on this Earth made of granite. I can help you someday if you miss your planet too much. I can...\"\n\n\"Oh! I understand very well,\" said the little prince, \"but why do you always speak in riddles?\"\n\n\"I solve them all,\" said the snake.\n\nAnd they were both silent.\n\nXVIII\n\nThe little prince crossed the desert and met only one flower. A flower with three petals, a flower of no importance at all...\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the flower.\n\n\"Where are the people?\" the little prince asked politely.\n\nThe flower had once seen a caravan go by:\n\n\"People? There are six or seven of them, I think. I caught sight of them years ago. But you never know where to find them. The wind blows them around. They have no roots, and that bothers them a lot.\"\n\n\"Goodbye,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Goodbye,\" said the flower.\n\nXIX\n\nThe little prince climbed a high mountain. The only mountains he had ever known were the three volcanoes that came up to his knee. And he used the extinct volcano as a footstool. \"From a mountain as high as this one,\" he said to himself, \"I will see the whole planet and all the people at once...\" But he saw nothing except sharp needles of rock.\n\n\"Good morning,\" he said, just in case.\n\n\"Good morning... Good morning... Good morning...\" answered the echo.\n\n\"Who are you?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Who are you... who are you... who are you...\" answered the echo.\n\n\"Be my friends, I am alone,\" he said.\n\n\"I am alone... I am alone... I am alone...\" answered the echo.\n\n\"What a strange planet!\" he thought then. \"It is all dry, and all pointed, and all salty.\n\n\"And the people have no imagination. They repeat what you say to them... At home I had a flower: she always spoke first...\"\n\nXX\n\nBut it happened that the little prince, after walking for a long time through sand, rocks and snow, at last came upon a road. And all roads go to where people are.\n\n\"Good morning,\" he said.\n\nIt was a garden full of blooming roses.\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the roses.\n\nThe little prince looked at them. They all looked like his flower.\n\n\"Who are you?\" he asked them, amazed.\n\n\"We are roses,\" said the roses.\n\n\"Oh!\" said the little prince...\n\nAnd he felt very unhappy. His flower had told him that she was the only one of her kind in the whole universe. And here were five thousand of them, all alike, in a single garden!\n\n\"She would be very hurt,\" he said to himself, \"if she saw this... she would cough terribly and pretend to be dying, so as not to look ridiculous. And I would have to pretend to nurse her back to health, because if I didn't, she would really let herself die, just to make me feel small too...\"\n\nThen he said to himself: \"I thought I was rich, with a flower like no other, and all I have is an ordinary rose. That, and my three volcanoes that come up to my knee, one of which may be extinct forever—that doesn't make me a very great prince...\" And, lying in the grass, he cried.\n\n[Picture 20-1: the little prince lying in the grass, crying]\n\nXXI\n\nIt was then that the fox appeared.\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the fox.\n\n\"Good morning,\" the little prince answered politely. He turned around but saw nothing.\n\n\"I'm here,\" said the voice, \"under the apple tree.\"\n\n\"Who are you?\" said the little prince. \"You are very pretty...\"\n\n\"I am a fox,\" said the fox.\n\n[Picture 21-1: a fox under an apple tree]\n\n\"Come and play with me,\" the little prince suggested. \"I am so sad...\"\n\n\"I can't play with you,\" said the fox. \"I am not tamed.\"\n\n\"Oh! Excuse me,\" said the little prince.\n\nBut after thinking it over, he added:\n\n\"What does 'tame' mean?\"\n\n\"You are not from around here,\" said the fox. \"What are you looking for?\"\n\n\"I am looking for people,\" said the little prince. \"What does 'tame' mean?\"\n\n\"People,\" said the fox, \"have guns and they go hunting. It's very annoying! They also raise chickens. That's the only interesting thing about them. Are you looking for chickens?\"\n\n\"No,\" said the little prince. \"I am looking for friends. What does 'tame' mean?\"\n\n\"It is something people have forgotten too much,\" said the fox. \"It means to make bonds...\"\n\n\"To make bonds?\"\n\n\"Of course,\" said the fox. \"To me, you are still only a little boy just like a hundred thousand other little boys. And I don't need you. And you don't need me either. To you, I am only a fox just like a hundred thousand other foxes. But if you tame me, we will need each other. To me, you will be the only one in the world. To you, I will be the only one in the world...\"\n\n\"I am beginning to understand,\" said the little prince. \"There is a flower... I think she has tamed me...\"\n\n\"That's possible,\" said the fox. \"You see all sorts of things on the Earth...\"\n\n\"Oh! It isn't on the Earth,\" said the little prince. The fox seemed very curious:\n\n\"On another planet?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Are there hunters on that planet?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Now that's interesting! And chickens?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Nothing is perfect,\" sighed the fox.\n\nBut the fox came back to his idea:\n\n\"My life is always the same. I hunt chickens; people hunt me. All chickens are alike, and all people are alike. So I get a little bored. But if you tame me, my life will be full of sunshine. I will know the sound of one set of footsteps that will be different from all the others. Other footsteps send me back under the ground. Yours will call me out of my den, like music. And look! Do you see the wheat fields over there? I don't eat bread. Wheat is no use to me. Wheat fields remind me of nothing. And that is sad! But you have hair the color of gold. So it will be wonderful once you have tamed me! The wheat, which is golden, will remind me of you. And I will love the sound of the wind in the wheat...\"\n\nThe fox stopped talking and looked at the little prince for a long time:\n\n\"Please... tame me!\" he said.\n\n\"I'd like to,\" answered the little prince, \"but I don't have much time. I have friends to find and a lot of things to get to know.\"\n\n\"You only know the things you tame,\" said the fox. \"People don't have time to get to know anything anymore. They buy things already made at stores. But since there are no stores that sell friends, people don't have friends anymore. If you want a friend, tame me!\"\n\n\"What do I have to do?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"You have to be very patient,\" answered the fox. \"First you will sit down a little way from me, like that, in the grass. I will look at you out of the corner of my eye, and you won't say anything. Words are where misunderstandings come from. But each day, you can sit a little closer...\"\n\nThe next day the little prince came back.\n\n\"It would have been better to come back at the same time,\" said the fox. \"If you come, for example, at four o'clock in the afternoon, then at three o'clock I will already start to be happy. The closer it gets to the time, the happier I will feel. At four o'clock, I will already be restless and worried; I will find out what happiness costs! But if you come at just any time, I will never know when to get my heart ready... We need rituals.\"\n\n\"What is a ritual?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"That is something else people have forgotten too much,\" said the fox. \"It is what makes one day different from the other days, and one hour different from the other hours. My hunters, for example, have a ritual. On Thursdays they dance with the girls from the village. So Thursday is a wonderful day! I go for a walk all the way to the vineyard. If the hunters danced at just any time, the days would all be alike, and I would never have a holiday.\"\n\nSo the little prince tamed the fox. And when the time for leaving came near:\n\n\"Ah!\" said the fox... \"I am going to cry.\"\n\n\"It's your own fault,\" said the little prince. \"I never wished you any harm, but you wanted me to tame you...\"\n\n\"Of course,\" said the fox.\n\n\"But you are going to cry!\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Of course,\" said the fox.\n\n\"Then you get nothing out of it!\"\n\n\"I do get something out of it,\" said the fox, \"because of the color of the wheat.\"\n\nThen he added:\n\n\"Go and look at the roses again. You will understand that yours is the only one in the world. Then come back and say goodbye to me, and I will give you a secret as a present.\"\n\nThe little prince went off to see the roses again.\n\n\"You are not at all like my rose. You are nothing yet,\" he told them. \"No one has tamed you, and you have not tamed anyone. You are the way my fox was. He was only a fox like a hundred thousand others. But I made him my friend, and now he is the only one in the world.\"\n\nAnd the roses were embarrassed.\n\n\"You are beautiful, but you are empty,\" he went on. \"No one could die for you. Of course, an ordinary passerby would think that my rose looked just like you. But she alone is more important than all of you together, because she is the one I watered. Because she is the one I put under the glass globe. Because she is the one I sheltered behind the screen. Because she is the one whose caterpillars I killed (except the two or three we kept for butterflies). Because she is the one I listened to when she complained, or boasted, or even sometimes when she said nothing at all. Because she is my rose.\"\n\nAnd he went back to the fox:\n\n\"Goodbye,\" he said...\n\n\"Goodbye,\" said the fox. \"Here is my secret. It is very simple: We see clearly only with the heart. What matters most cannot be seen with the eyes.\"\n\n\"What matters most cannot be seen with the eyes,\" the little prince repeated, so that he would remember.\n\n\"It is the time you gave to your rose that makes your rose so important.\"\n\n\"It is the time I gave to my rose...\" said the little prince, so that he would remember.\n\n\"People have forgotten this truth,\" said the fox. \"But you must not forget it. You are responsible forever for what you have tamed. You are responsible for your rose...\"\n\n\"I am responsible for my rose...\" the little prince repeated, so that he would remember.\n\nXXII\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the switchman.\n\n\"What are you doing here?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"I sort the travelers, in bundles of a thousand,\" said the switchman. \"I send off the trains that carry them, sometimes to the right, sometimes to the left.\"\n\nAnd a fast train, all lit up and rumbling like thunder, made the switchman's cabin shake.\n\n\"They are in a big hurry,\" said the little prince. \"What are they looking for?\"\n\n\"Even the man who drives the engine doesn't know,\" said the switchman.\n\nAnd a second fast train, all lit up, rumbled past in the other direction.\n\n\"Are they coming back already?\" asked the little prince...\n\n\"They aren't the same ones,\" said the switchman. \"It's an exchange.\"\n\n\"Weren't they happy where they were?\"\n\n\"Nobody is ever happy where they are,\" said the switchman.\n\nAnd the thunder of a third lit-up fast train rumbled by.\n\n\"Are they chasing the first travelers?\" asked the little prince.\n\n\"They aren't chasing anything at all,\" said the switchman. \"They sleep in there, or else they yawn. Only the children press their noses against the windows.\"\n\n\"Only the children know what they are looking for,\" said the little prince. \"They spend their time on a rag doll, and it becomes very important, and if someone takes it away from them, they cry...\"\n\n\"They are lucky,\" said the switchman.\n\nXXIII\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the little prince.\n\n\"Good morning,\" said the merchant. He was a merchant who sold improved pills that take away thirst. You swallow one a week, and you no longer feel any need to drink.\n\n\"Why do you sell those?\" said the little prince.\n\n\"They save a great deal of time,\" said the merchant. \"The experts have done the math. You save fifty-three minutes a week.\"\n\n\"And what do you do with the fifty-three minutes?\"\n\n\"Whatever you like...\"\n\n\"As for me,\" the little prince said to himself, \"if I had fifty-three minutes to spend, I would walk very slowly toward a spring of water...\"\n\nXXIV\n\nIt was now the eighth day since my breakdown in the desert, and I had listened to the story of the merchant while drinking the last drop of my water supply.\n\n\"Ah,\" I said to the little prince, \"these memories of yours are very pretty, but I still haven't repaired my plane, I have nothing left to drink, and I would be happy too if I could walk very slowly toward a spring of water!\"\n\n\"My friend the fox,\" he said to me...\n\n\"My little fellow, this has nothing to do with the fox anymore!\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"Because we are going to die of thirst...\"\n\nHe did not follow my reasoning. He answered me:\n\n\"It's good to have had a friend, even if you are going to die. I am very glad that I had a fox for a friend...\"\n\nHe doesn't see the danger, I said to myself. He is never hungry or thirsty. A little sunshine is all he needs...\n\nBut he looked at me and answered my thought:\n\n\"I am thirsty too... let's look for a well...\"\n\nI made a tired gesture: it is foolish to look for a well, at random, in the vastness of the desert. All the same, we set off walking.\n\nAfter we had walked for hours in silence, night fell, and the stars began to light up. I saw them as if in a dream, since I had a slight fever from my thirst. The little prince's words danced in my memory.\n\n\"So you are thirsty too?\" I asked him.\n\nBut he did not answer my question. He simply said to me:\n\n\"Water can be good for the heart too...\"\n\nI did not understand his answer, but I kept quiet... I knew very well that I should not question him.\n\nHe was tired. He sat down. I sat down beside him. And after a silence, he spoke again:\n\n\"The stars are beautiful because of a flower that you cannot see...\"\n\nI answered, \"Of course,\" and without speaking I looked at the ripples of the sand under the moon.\n\n\"The desert is beautiful,\" he added...\n\nAnd it was true. I have always loved the desert. You sit down on a sand dune. You see nothing. You hear nothing. And yet something shines out in the silence...\n\n\"What makes the desert beautiful,\" said the little prince, \"is that it hides a well somewhere...\"\n\nI was surprised to understand, all of a sudden, that mysterious glow of the sand. When I was a little boy, I lived in an old house, and the story went that a treasure was buried there. Of course, no one ever managed to find it, and perhaps no one even looked for it. But it cast a spell over the whole house. My house was hiding a secret deep in its heart...\n\n\"Yes,\" I said to the little prince, \"whether it's the house, the stars or the desert, what makes them beautiful cannot be seen!\"\n\n\"I am glad,\" he said, \"that you agree with my fox.\"\n\nAs the little prince was falling asleep, I took him in my arms and set off again. I was deeply moved. I felt as if I were carrying a fragile treasure. It even seemed to me that there was nothing more fragile on all the Earth. In the moonlight I looked at that pale forehead, those closed eyes, those locks of hair trembling in the wind, and I said to myself: \"What I see here is only a shell. The most important part cannot be seen...\"\n\nAs his half-open lips began a faint smile, I said to myself again: \"What moves me so much about this little prince asleep is his loyalty to a flower, the image of a rose that shines inside him like the flame of a lamp, even when he is asleep...\" And I sensed that he was even more fragile than I had thought. Lamps must be well protected: a gust of wind can blow them out...\n\nAnd walking on like that, I found the well at daybreak.\n\nXXV\n\n\"People,\" said the little prince, \"rush off into express trains, but they no longer know what they are looking for. So they fuss about and go around in circles...\"\n\nAnd he added:\n\n\"It isn't worth it...\"\n\nThe well we had reached was not like the other wells of the Sahara. The wells of the Sahara are simple holes dug in the sand. This one looked like a village well. But there was no village anywhere around, and I thought I must be dreaming.\n\n\"It's strange,\" I said to the little prince. \"Everything is ready: the pulley, the bucket and the rope...\"\n\nHe laughed, touched the rope and set the pulley turning. And the pulley groaned like an old weather vane when the wind has been asleep for a long time.\n\n\"Do you hear?\" said the little prince. \"We are waking this well up, and it is singing...\"\n\nI did not want him to strain himself.\n\n\"Let me do it,\" I said to him. \"It's too heavy for you.\"\n\nSlowly I hoisted the bucket up to the rim of the well. I set it there, good and steady. The song of the pulley went on in my ears, and in the water, which was still trembling, I saw the sun tremble.\n\n\"I am thirsty for this water,\" said the little prince. \"Give me some to drink...\"\n\nAnd I understood what he had been looking for!\n\nI lifted the bucket to his lips. He drank with his eyes closed. It was as sweet as a holiday. This water was something far more than a drink. It was born of the walk under the stars, of the song of the pulley, of the work of my arms. It was good for the heart, like a present. In the same way, when I was a little boy, the lights of the Christmas tree, the music of midnight Mass and the gentleness of smiling faces were what made the Christmas present I received shine so brightly.\n\n\"The people where you live,\" said the little prince, \"grow five thousand roses in one single garden... and they don't find what they are looking for there...\"\n\n\"They don't find it,\" I answered...\n\n\"And yet what they are looking for could be found in one single rose, or in a little water...\"\n\n\"Of course,\" I answered.\n\nAnd the little prince added:\n\n\"But eyes are blind. You have to look with the heart.\"\n\nI had had my drink. I could breathe easily. At daybreak, the sand is the color of honey. That honey color made me happy too. Why, then, did I have to feel this sorrow...\n\n\"You must keep your promise,\" the little prince said to me gently. He had sat down beside me again.\n\n\"What promise?\"\n\n\"You know... a muzzle for my sheep... I am responsible for that flower!\"\n\nI took my rough sketches out of my pocket. The little prince caught sight of them and said, laughing:\n\n\"Your baobabs look a bit like cabbages...\"\n\n\"Oh!\"\n\nAnd I had been so proud of my baobabs!\n\n\"Your fox... his ears... they look a bit like horns... and they are too long!\"\n\nAnd he laughed again.\n\n\"You're being unfair, little fellow. I didn't know how to draw anything except boa constrictors from the outside and boa constrictors from the inside.\"\n\n\"Oh, it will be all right,\" he said. \"Children understand.\"\n\nSo I sketched a muzzle in pencil. And my heart ached as I gave it to him.\n\n\"You have plans that I don't know about...\"\n\nBut he did not answer me. He said:\n\n\"You know, my fall to Earth... tomorrow it will be one year...\"\n\nThen, after a silence, he went on:\n\n\"I landed very close to here...\"\n\nAnd he blushed.\n\nAnd once again, without understanding why, I felt a strange sadness. All the same, a question came to me:\n\n\"So it wasn't by chance that, on the morning I met you, eight days ago, you were walking around like that, all alone, a thousand miles from any place where people live! You were going back to the spot where you fell?\"\n\nThe little prince blushed again.\n\nAnd I added, hesitating:\n\n\"Because of the anniversary, perhaps?...\"\n\nThe little prince blushed once more. He never answered questions, but when someone blushes, that means \"yes,\" doesn't it?\n\n\"Ah,\" I said to him, \"I'm afraid...\"\n\nBut he answered me:\n\n\"Now you must go to work. You must go back to your machine. I'll wait for you here. Come back tomorrow evening...\"\n\nBut I did not feel reassured. I remembered the fox. When you let yourself be tamed, you run the risk of crying a little...\n\nXXVI\n\nBeside the well there was the ruin of an old stone wall. When I came back from my work the next evening, I saw my little prince from far away, sitting up on top of it with his legs dangling. And I heard him talking:\n\n\"So you don't remember?\" he was saying. \"It isn't exactly here!\"\n\nAnother voice must have answered him, because he replied:\n\n\"Yes! Yes! It's the right day, but this isn't the right place...\"\n\nI kept walking toward the wall. I still could not see or hear anyone. Yet the little prince replied again:\n\n\"...Of course. You'll see where my tracks begin in the sand. You only have to wait for me there. I'll be there tonight...\"\n\nI was twenty meters from the wall, and I still could not see anything.\n\nAfter a silence, the little prince spoke again:\n\n\"Is your poison good? Are you sure you won't make me suffer for long?\"\n\nI stopped, my heart aching, but I still did not understand.\n\n\"Now go away,\" he said... \"I want to get down!\"\n\nThen I looked down myself at the foot of the wall, and I jumped! There, raised up toward the little prince, was one of those yellow snakes that can kill you in thirty seconds. While I dug in my pocket to pull out my revolver, I broke into a run, but at the noise I made, the snake let himself slide gently into the sand, like a fountain's spray dying away, and without hurrying too much, he slipped in between the stones with a faint metallic sound.\n\nI reached the wall just in time to catch my little fellow of a prince in my arms. He was as pale as snow.\n\n\"What is all this about? Now you're talking with snakes!\"\n\nI had loosened the golden scarf he always wore. I had wet his temples and given him something to drink. And now I did not dare ask him anything more. He looked at me seriously and put his arms around my neck. I could feel his heart beating like the heart of a dying bird that has been shot with a rifle. He said to me:\n\n\"I'm glad you have found what was wrong with your machine. Now you'll be able to go home...\"\n\n\"How do you know?\"\n\nI had come just then to tell him that, against all hope, my work had been a success!\n\nHe did not answer my question, but he added:\n\n\"I'm going home today too...\"\n\nThen, sadly:\n\n\"It's much farther... it's much harder...\"\n\nI could tell that something extraordinary was happening. I was holding him in my arms like a little child, and yet it seemed to me that he was sliding straight down into a deep pit, and that I could do nothing to hold him back...\n\nHis gaze was serious, lost somewhere far away:\n\n\"I have your sheep. And I have the box for the sheep. And I have the muzzle...\"\n\nAnd he gave a sad smile.\n\nI waited a long time. I could feel him slowly growing warmer.\n\n\"Little fellow, you were scared...\"\n\nOf course he had been scared! But he laughed softly:\n\n\"I'll be much more scared tonight...\"\n\nOnce again I felt myself go cold with the feeling that something could never be undone. And I understood that I could not bear the thought of never hearing that laugh again. For me it was like a spring in the desert.\n\n\"Little fellow, I want to hear you laugh again...\"\n\nBut he said to me:\n\n\"Tonight it will be one year. My star will be right above the place where I fell last year...\"\n\n\"Little fellow, tell me this is just a bad dream, all this business about a snake and a meeting place and a star...\"\n\nBut he did not answer my question. He said to me:\n\n\"What is important can't be seen...\"\n\n\"Of course...\"\n\n\"It's like the flower. If you love a flower that lives on a star, it's sweet to look at the sky at night. All the stars are in bloom.\"\n\n\"Of course...\"\n\n\"It's like the water. The water you gave me to drink was like music, because of the pulley and the rope... you remember... it was good.\"\n\n\"Of course...\"\n\n\"At night you'll look up at the stars. Where I live is too small for me to show you where mine is. It's better that way. For you, my star will be one of the stars. So you will love looking at all the stars... They will all be your friends. And then I'm going to give you a present...\"\n\nHe laughed again.\n\n\"Ah, little fellow, little fellow, I love to hear that laugh!\"\n\n\"That's exactly what my present will be... it will be like the water...\"\n\n\"What do you mean?\"\n\n\"People have stars, but they aren't the same stars. For some people, the ones who travel, the stars are guides. For others they are nothing but little lights. For others, who are scholars, they are problems to solve. For my businessman they were gold. But all those stars are silent. You, you will have stars like nobody else has...\"\n\n\"What do you mean?\"\n\n\"When you look at the sky at night, since I will be living on one of them, since I will be laughing on one of them, then for you it will be as if all the stars were laughing. You will have stars that know how to laugh!\"\n\nAnd he laughed again.\n\n\"And when you have been comforted (people always get over their sorrow in the end), you'll be glad you knew me. You will always be my friend. You'll want to laugh with me. And sometimes you'll open your window, just like that, for the fun of it... And your friends will be very surprised to see you laughing while you look at the sky. Then you'll tell them, 'Yes, the stars always make me laugh!' And they'll think you're crazy. I'll have played a very mean trick on you...\"\n\nAnd he laughed again.\n\n\"It will be as if I had given you, instead of stars, heaps of little bells that know how to laugh...\"\n\nAnd he laughed again. Then he turned serious once more:\n\n\"Tonight... you know... don't come.\"\n\n\"I won't leave you.\"\n\n\"I'll look as if I'm hurting... I'll look a little as if I'm dying. That's how it is. Don't come to see that. It isn't worth it...\"\n\n\"I won't leave you.\"\n\nBut he was worried.\n\n\"I'm telling you this... it's also because of the snake. He mustn't bite you... Snakes are nasty. They can bite just for fun...\"\n\n\"I won't leave you.\"\n\nBut something made him feel better:\n\n\"It's true that they don't have any poison left for the second bite...\"\n\nThat night I did not see him set off. He had slipped away without a sound. When I managed to catch up with him, he was walking with quick, determined steps. He only said to me:\n\n\"Ah! You're here...\"\n\nAnd he took me by the hand. But he was still troubled:\n\n\"You were wrong to come. You'll be sad. I'll look as if I'm dead, and it won't be true...\"\n\nI said nothing.\n\n\"You understand. It's too far. I can't carry this body with me. It's too heavy.\"\n\nI said nothing.\n\n\"But it will be like an old shell left behind. There's nothing sad about old shells...\"\n\nI said nothing.\n\nHe lost heart a little. But he made one more effort:\n\n\"It will be nice, you know. I'll be looking at the stars too. All the stars will be wells with a rusty pulley. All the stars will pour me water to drink...\"\n\nI said nothing.\n\n\"It will be so much fun! You'll have five hundred million little bells, and I'll have five hundred million springs...\"\n\nAnd then he fell silent too, because he was crying...\n\n\"It's here. Let me take one step by myself.\"\n\nAnd he sat down, because he was afraid.\n\nHe spoke again:\n\n\"You know... my flower... I'm responsible for her! And she is so weak! And she is so innocent. She has four tiny thorns that are worth nothing to protect her against the world...\"\n\nI sat down, because I could no longer stand. He said:\n\n\"There... That's all...\"\n\nHe hesitated a little longer, then he stood up. He took one step. I could not move.\n\nThere was nothing but a yellow flash near his ankle. For a moment he stayed perfectly still. He did not cry out. He fell gently, the way a tree falls. There was not even a sound, because of the sand.\n\n[Picture 26-1: the little prince falling gently onto the soft sand, like a tree falling, under a starry sky]\n\nXXVII\n\nAnd now, of course, it has already been six years... I have never told this story before. The friends who saw me again were very glad to see me alive. I was sad, but I told them, \"It's just that I'm tired...\"\n\nNow I have been comforted a little. That is to say... not completely. But I know very well that he went back to his planet, because at daybreak I did not find his body. It was not such a heavy body... And at night I love to listen to the stars. It is like five hundred million little bells...\n\nBut now something extraordinary is happening. The muzzle I drew for the little prince — I forgot to add the leather strap to it! He will never have been able to fasten it onto the sheep. So I wonder, \"What has happened on his planet? Maybe the sheep has eaten the flower...\"\n\nSometimes I tell myself, \"Surely not! The little prince shuts his flower under her glass globe every night, and he keeps a close watch on his sheep...\" Then I am happy. And all the stars laugh softly.\n\nOther times I tell myself, \"Everyone gets careless once in a while, and that is all it takes! One evening he forgot the glass globe, or else the sheep crept out without a sound during the night...\" Then the little bells all turn into tears!...\n\nThat is a very great mystery. For you, who love the little prince too, just as for me, nothing in the universe is the same if somewhere, no one knows where, a sheep that we have never met has, yes or no, eaten a rose...\n\nLook at the sky. Ask yourselves: has the sheep eaten the flower, yes or no? And you will see how everything changes...\n\nAnd no grown-up will ever understand that this matters so much!\n\nThis, for me, is the loveliest and the saddest landscape in the world. It is the same landscape as the one on the previous page, but I have drawn it once more to show it to you clearly. This is where the little prince appeared on Earth, and then disappeared.\n\n[Picture 27-1: a lonely desert landscape under a star, the place where the little prince appeared on Earth and then disappeared]\n\nLook carefully at this landscape so that you will be sure to recognize it, if one day you travel to Africa, in the desert. And if you ever happen to pass that way, I beg you, don't hurry on — wait a little while, right under the star! Then if a child comes up to you, if he laughs, if he has golden hair, if he does not answer when you ask him questions, you will easily guess who he is. Then please be kind! Don't leave me so sad: write to me quickly and tell me that he has come back...\n"};
